=== An allegory
--- These Things Be an Allegory
kicker: Application • The life of Sarah
sub: Faithful Believing Israel
p: Now, as the Bible in **Galatians 4** says, regarding the story of Sarah and Isaac, that these things be an allegory, the children of the promise in Sarah, the children after the bondage of the law, in Hagar. That the true seed of Abraham isn't after the flesh, as Ishmael was by the bondwoman Hagar, but by the miracle of God, Isaac, conceived in faith through Sarah.
p: The life story of Sarah represents Old Testament Israel, faithful believing Israel, who were saved through faith in the promise made to Abraham. An allegory, as the Bible says.

--- Barren, and into Egypt
kicker: Application • The life of Sarah
sub: A Mixed Multitude
p: When we first meet Sarah, as a young wife to Abraham, we are told she was barren. Without hope of conception. She journeys to Canaan, the promised land.
p: Later, due to famine, she sojourns into Egypt, is captured by Pharaoh, and God for Sarah's sake visits plagues upon the Pharaoh's house until he relents and sends Sarah, Abraham, Lot and their cattle and belongings out from Egypt. Just as the Israelites spoiled Egypt, and went out with them, a mixed multitude.

--- My Wrong Be upon Thee
kicker: Application • The life of Sarah
sub: Impatient with God's Promise
p: Sarah, despite her faith and love of God lapses in faith, and brings adultery to the bed of Abraham, with Hagar. Sarah's impatience with God's promise has her seek to find an heir after the flesh. Just as the Israelites lapsed in faith and brought the adultery of strange gods at times, and eventually sought an earthly king to rule them, frustrated at the timing of God.
p: And like Israel, which would at times repent and turn back to God, admitting their error, Sarah says to Abraham; *My wrong be upon thee: I have given my maid into thy bosom; and when she saw that she had conceived, I was despised in her eyes: the Lord judge between me and thee.* [[Genesis 16:5]]

--- The Heir after the Law
kicker: Application • The life of Sarah
sub: Another Captivity
p: And Hagar, having fled from Sarah with Ishmael the heir after the law, representing Israel after the flesh, not of faith, was given another chance by God, so too were the Jews of Jesus earthly ministry, to receive the adoption by faith that they thought was theirs by law. And Sarah, believing Israel, gave them a home until the coming of Isaac.
p: We are told of another captivity, Sarah taken by a king, and freed not by plagues but by the fear of the Lord upon Abimelech, convicted of his error, just as Babylonian captivity was.

--- Cast Out the Bondwoman
kicker: Application • The life of Sarah
sub: The Non-Believing Jews Were Cut Off
p: And when Isaac was born, Ishmael mocked the heir of the promise, and Sarah cast out the bondwoman and her son, though it grieved Abraham, the father. But God agrees with Sarah that son after the flesh, the heir according to the law be cast out, so that the son of faith may come in to his inheritance in full. Just as the non-believing Jews were cut off.
p: (God, in His mercy offers salvation to Hagar if she just believe. And a well of water giving her salvation, just as any seed of Abraham after the flesh can still repent towards Christ today)

=== Do ye not hear the law?
--- The Righteous Anger of Paul
kicker: Application • Galatians
sub: Another Gospel
p: And so with this better understanding of the allegory, of recognizing that the entire Old Testament is about Christ Jesus and salvation by faith alone in him, to redeem us, offered by God the father in love and grace, we can understand the righteous anger of Paul writing unto the Galatians.
p: The Church of the Galatians being swayed by judaizers into accepting another gospel, one of bondage to the law. Whereby they were being told that they must circumcise, keep the days and the months and the dietary laws in the carnal ordinances to be saved. Being dragged back to Hagar, the Bond woman and rejecting Sarah and the son of the promise. Of faith.

--- O Foolish Galatians
kicker: Cross references • Galatians 3–4
fig: Galatians 3:1 | Galatians 3:1
fig: Galatians 4:19-20 | Galatians 4:19-20
callout: The Apostle is angry, he says he fears them. He says that he doubts their salvation, which he had labored with them and questions if it was in vain. Paul saying that only an unsaved person who didn't believe the gospel of Faith he presented them would be susceptible to the judaizers dragging them back under the law and away from the liberty of Christ.

--- Do Ye Not Hear the Law?
kicker: Cross references • Galatians 4
fig: Galatians 4:21-22 | Galatians 4:21-22
fig: Galatians 4:23-24 | Galatians 4:23-24
callout: He says that he's willing to go through this again with them that they may come to Jesus. That they seeking to be brought back under the law, he says don't you even hear the law? And this is what prompts his use of Sarah and Hagar as an allegory. As a teaching moment taken from scripture that they may understand the difference between salvation by faith alone and the bondage of the law.

--- The Seed after Hagar
kicker: Application • Galatians
sub: The Same Message
p: This is the same message we can give to those claiming Christ who have added works of the law to the salvation given in the simple Gospel of reconciliation and faith.
p: These judaizers and those bewitched by them will claim that, "Yes it's by faith alone but you have to do the works of the law to be saved. You have to adhere to our vain traditions. To our carnal ordinances. To our dietary restrictions. You don't expect to just be handed salvation? Do you?"
p: And so they bring people to be the seed after Hagar, not Sarah. And like Ishmael, they do not receive the inheritance which is given by faith in God's promise, not by heirs according to the law.

--- A Free Gift
kicker: Application • Galatians
sub: Hear the Law
p: Just as Sarah was barren from birth, and brought to old age, it took a miracle of God, visited upon her, to bring her the promise. Not by anything she did, for she was not capable, but in God all things are possible.
p: If you're trusting in your own works, if you're sewing your own fig leaves, if you're offering the fruit of your garden, instead of the firstling of the flock, if you're committing adultery with Hagar to produce your salvation, repent. Hear the law.
p: Salvation is given us by God, in His grace, through His Son, Jesus Christ, killed for our sins, a burnt offering, raised from the dead, that we may be redeemed in faith and sealed by the Holy Spirit unto the day of redemption. A free gift that once accepted cannot be lost. Eternal life. Heir to the Kingdom. Sons of God.

--- This Is the Lesson
center: This is the lesson. *Do you not hear the law?*
